            <ArticleTitle>Lost productive time and costs due to diabetes and diabetic neuropathic pain in the US workforce.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>672-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Estimate the impact of diabetes and neuropathic pain on the US workforce.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Data on lost productive time (LPT) was collected by telephone interview in a random sample of the US population (N=36,634). Of 19,075 occupation-eligible working adults included in the analysis, 1003 reported a physician diagnosis of diabetes; 38% of these reported numbness or tingling in feet or hands due to diabetes (symptom group). We compared diabetes respondents with and without symptoms to other respondents for LPT and related cost.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Health-related LPT was 18% higher in the symptom (P&lt;0.05) and 5% higher in the non-symptom (P&lt;0.05) groups versus for those without diabetes. The symptom group lost 1.4 hours of work per week more than the non-symptom group (P&lt;0.05).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Workers who have diabetes with neuropathic symptoms lose the equivalent of $3.65 billion/yr in health-related LPT.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
